  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R8-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague expects daily updates, but nothing meaningful changes every day. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Agree an update rhythm based on useful changes and decision needs$qb$, $qb$Ask what information they need and how often they use it$qb$, $qb$Keep sending empty daily updates$qb$, $qb$Stop updates until something changes$qb$, $qb$Tell them daily updates are unnecessary$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A junior colleague fixes an error after you point it out but does not understand why it happened. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Help them identify the cause and the check that would prevent the same error next time$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain what they will do differently next time$qb$, $qb$Tell them to be more careful$qb$, $qb$Check all their future work yourself$qb$, $qb$Move the task to someone else$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks permission for a routine choice that is clearly within the authority you gave them. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm that they can make the choice and reinforce the agreed decision limits$qb$, $qb$Remind them of their authority and ask them to proceed$qb$, $qb$Approve the choice this time$qb$, $qb$Take over the decision because they asked$qb$, $qb$Require approval for all future routine choices$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A proposed task would improve a minor internal process but delay an important customer commitment. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Protect the customer commitment and schedule the internal improvement when capacity allows$qb$, $qb$Explain the trade-off and defer the internal task$qb$, $qb$Try to do both at the same time$qb$, $qb$Do the internal improvement because it is quick$qb$, $qb$Delay the customer work without discussion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-0TO2-R9-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A person asks for more detail in every update, but you are unsure what they actually use. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what decisions they make from the update and tailor the detail to that need$qb$, $qb$Ask which sections they want expanded$qb$, $qb$Add more detail to every update$qb$, $qb$Send the full working file instead$qb$, $qb$Tell them the current update is sufficient$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What coaching strategy is most effective when supporting a junior team member struggling with complex tasks?$qb$, $qb$Remediating technical skill gaps by reviewing work together, modeling shortcuts, and setting review milestones.$qb$, $qb$Providing completed past work samples for the junior staff member to reference during future tasks.$qb$, $qb$Reassigning complex tasks to experienced team members while assigning simpler duties to the junior staff member.$qb$, $qb$Encouraging the junior staff member to work faster during task execution to improve turnaround times.$qb$, $qb$Completing final quality checks personally to ensure client deliverables remain on schedule.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How can a project coordinator effectively empower team members when delegating operational tasks?$qb$, $qb$Instituting clear outcome expectations, granting operational execution autonomy, and holding milestone check-ins.$qb$, $qb$Providing detailed step-by-step formatting rules and reviewing execution work daily to ensure exact compliance.$qb$, $qb$Asking for task volunteers and leaving layout design and execution entirely to their discretion.$qb$, $qb$Creating operational templates personally and delegating only basic routine data entry steps to team members.$qb$, $qb$Handing over tasks completely without scheduling progress reviews to demonstrate trust.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How does an operations lead align daily team routines with new corporate strategic priorities?$qb$, $qb$Translating strategic goals into practical workflow adjustments and setting measurable operational targets.$qb$, $qb$Reminding team members during group meetings to keep strategic priorities in mind during task execution.$qb$, $qb$Awaiting specific step-by-step operational instructions from executive management before altering team workflows.$qb$, $qb$Sharing corporate strategy announcements in team communication channels to promote general awareness.$qb$, $qb$Focusing team effort exclusively on core local targets, assuming strategy is managed by executive leads.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How should an associate handle recurring custom report requests from internal stakeholders that overload capacity?$qb$, $qb$Uncovering underlying decision-making needs, proposing streamlined standard formats, and agreeing on cadence.$qb$, $qb$Fulfilling all custom report requests as submitted to maintain positive inter-departmental relationships.$qb$, $qb$Declining custom requests firmly, citing department capacity constraints and standard reporting policies.$qb$, $qb$Escalating custom reporting demands to supervisors to negotiate delivery limits with stakeholders.$qb$, $qb$Delivering requested custom reports at reduced frequencies without discussing schedule adjustments.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-045$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$How does a team lead build psychological safety when a team member discloses an operational oversight during a post-mortem?$qb$, $qb$Reframing the oversight constructively as a collective learning opportunity to improve systemic safeguards.$qb$, $qb$Thanking the team member briefly for admitting the oversight and moving swiftly to the next meeting agenda item.$qb$, $qb$Reminding the team member in front of peers regarding the importance of double-checking work to avoid delays.$qb$, $qb$Scheduling a private follow-up meeting with the team member after the post-mortem to discuss performance impacts.$qb$, $qb$Requiring team lead sign-off on all future deliverables from junior staff to prevent similar oversights.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R1-046$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$What is the most effective strategy to eliminate recurring workflow delays between interfacing departments?$qb$, $qb$Co-designing shared workflow processes and binding service-level agreements (SLAs) collaboratively with partner leads.$qb$, $qb$Instructing internal team members to submit task requests earlier to accommodate partner department delays.$qb$, $qb$Escalating recurring inter-departmental delays to senior executives to enforce compliance with schedules.$qb$, $qb$Sending regular reminder notifications to partner department approvers whenever task deadlines approach.$qb$, $qb$Bypassing standard cross-departmental channels by requesting emergency execution overrides from supervisors.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A capable junior colleague keeps bringing you decisions they could make themselves. What is the best coaching response?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the decision boundaries, ask for their recommendation, and let them decide within those limits$qb$, $qb$Ask them to bring a recommended option rather than only the problem$qb$, $qb$Continue deciding for them until their confidence improves$qb$, $qb$Tell them to stop asking questions$qb$, $qb$Give them full freedom without clarifying limits$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A delegated task is important, and the colleague has the skill but limited experience with this context. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Delegate the outcome and authority clearly, agree risk-based check-ins, and let them own the work$qb$, $qb$Give them the task with clear success criteria and one or two review points$qb$, $qb$Give detailed instructions for every step$qb$, $qb$Keep the important decisions yourself$qb$, $qb$Do the task yourself because the context is new to them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team has several good improvement ideas but limited capacity. How should you choose?$qb$, $qb$Prioritize ideas by contribution to the agreed goals, expected value, effort, and risk$qb$, $qb$Rank ideas by expected impact on the main goals$qb$, $qb$Choose the easiest ideas to create quick wins$qb$, $qb$Choose the ideas proposed by senior stakeholders$qb$, $qb$Try to start all ideas so none are rejected$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A stakeholder asks for a weekly report that takes significant effort, but you suspect they use only one part of it. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Understand the decision need, agree the minimum useful information and cadence, and redesign the report accordingly$qb$, $qb$Ask which sections they actually use and simplify the report$qb$, $qb$Continue the full report because the stakeholder requested it$qb$, $qb$Stop the report until they explain why they need it$qb$, $qb$Send raw data instead of the report$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-045$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate raises a concern about a plan supported by senior people and seems worried about speaking openly. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Invite the evidence, examine the concern fairly, and make clear that raising a genuine risk is useful regardless of senior support$qb$, $qb$Thank them for raising the concern and ask them to explain the risk$qb$, $qb$Tell them to raise it privately with the senior sponsor$qb$, $qb$Ask them to wait until the plan shows signs of failure$qb$, $qb$Discourage the concern because the plan already has senior approval$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R2-046$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Two teams meet their own targets, but their handoff creates repeated delays for customers. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Map the shared flow, agree a common outcome and handoff measures, and redesign ownership across the boundary$qb$, $qb$Bring both teams together to identify where the handoff creates delay$qb$, $qb$Ask each team to improve its own target performance further$qb$, $qb$Escalate the slower team to management$qb$, $qb$Leave the handoff unchanged because both teams meet their targets$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R3-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A junior teammate gives the right answer but relied on a guess. The goal is to build judgement rather than create dependence on you. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to work through the reasoning so they can repeat the judgement reliably$qb$, $qb$Ask what information they would check next time$qb$, $qb$Praise the correct answer and move on$qb$, $qb$Tell them guessing is unacceptable$qb$, $qb$Make future decisions for them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
