  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R9-046$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team and another team keep using different names for the same status, causing handoff errors. Both teams meet local measures while the shared outcome suffers. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Agree shared status definitions and use them in both teams$qb$, $qb$Compare the two sets of terms and identify the differences$qb$, $qb$Ask your team to learn the other team's terms$qb$, $qb$Add explanations each time a handoff occurs$qb$, $qb$Keep your own terms because changing them will take time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A junior colleague asks the same process question for the third time. The aim is to build independent judgement. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to walk through the process, identify the gap, and practice the step$qb$, $qb$Explain the step again and ask them to write it down$qb$, $qb$Give them the answer each time until they remember it$qb$, $qb$Tell them they should already know it$qb$, $qb$Do the step yourself to save time$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a task and the colleague proposes a different method that still meets the requirements. The colleague needs meaningful authority within clear boundaries. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Let them use the method if it stays within the agreed limits and outcome$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the method and confirm the expected result$qb$, $qb$Require your method because you delegated the task$qb$, $qb$Take the task back to avoid risk$qb$, $qb$Approve every step before they continue$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team activity is popular but uses time needed for a higher-priority goal. The request competes with an agreed priority. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Explain the trade-off and reduce the activity so effort supports the priority$qb$, $qb$Review whether the activity can be shortened or moved$qb$, $qb$Keep both activities and ask people to work faster$qb$, $qb$Protect the popular activity because morale matters$qb$, $qb$Ignore the priority until the next planning cycle$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A person affected by your work has not replied to two important questions. The stakeholder needs enough notice to adjust their plan. What is the best next step?$qb$, $qb$Use another appropriate contact method, explain the impact, and agree a response time$qb$, $qb$Send a clear follow-up showing what is blocked$qb$, $qb$Keep sending the same reminder each day$qb$, $qb$Continue the work using your own assumptions$qb$, $qb$Wait until they contact you$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-045$qb$, $qb$A5-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate points out a problem in your plan during a group discussion. The concern challenges a plan supported by senior people. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them, explore the concern, and change the plan if the evidence supports it$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the concern and consider it$qb$, $qb$Defend the plan so the group does not lose confidence$qb$, $qb$Tell them to raise concerns privately next time$qb$, $qb$Dismiss the concern because the plan is already approved$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-2TO5-R10-046$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Another team needs information your team already has, but nobody owns the handoff. Both teams meet local measures while the shared result suffers. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Agree who will provide the information now and define the handoff for future cases$qb$, $qb$Send the information and tell both teams that ownership needs clarification$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to submit a formal request$qb$, $qb$Wait for a manager to assign an owner$qb$, $qb$Keep the information within your team because no handoff is defined$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R2-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A junior colleague fixes an error after you point it out but does not understand why it happened. The colleague performs well in familiar cases but needs judgement that transfers to new situations. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Help them identify the cause and the check that would prevent the same error next time$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain what they will do differently next time$qb$, $qb$Tell them to be more careful$qb$, $qb$Check all their future work yourself$qb$, $qb$Move the task to someone else$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R2-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks permission for a routine choice that is clearly within the authority you gave them. The delegated outcome involves several stakeholders and decisions that should not all return to you. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm that they can make the choice and reinforce the agreed decision limits$qb$, $qb$Remind them of their authority and ask them to proceed$qb$, $qb$Approve the choice this time$qb$, $qb$Take over the decision because they asked$qb$, $qb$Require approval for all future routine choices$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
