  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A change in your work will delay another team's activity by one day. The stakeholder's changes partly reflect real market movement but are also creating avoidable churn across multiple teams. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the affected team early, explain the impact, and agree the next step$qb$, $qb$Tell them as soon as the new date is fully confirmed$qb$, $qb$Update your manager and let them inform the other team$qb$, $qb$Wait until the other team asks about the delay$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because the delay is only one day$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-046$qb$, $qb$A5-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A task is stuck because two teams each think the other team owns the next step. Every function is meeting its own scorecard, yet the combined process is worsening customer outcomes and total business cost. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Bring both sides together, clarify the handoff, and agree one owner$qb$, $qb$Contact the other team and ask who can take the next step$qb$, $qb$Escalate the delay to both managers immediately$qb$, $qb$Complete the other team's step yourself without agreement$qb$, $qb$Wait until one team accepts responsibility$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new teammate completes a task correctly but takes much longer than expected. You need the person to handle unfamiliar high-impact decisions without creating dependence on your approval. What is the best coaching response?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain their process, then help identify where time can be saved$qb$, $qb$Show them one faster method and let them practice it$qb$, $qb$Tell them they need to work faster next time$qb$, $qb$Take over the task whenever speed matters$qb$, $qb$Compare their speed publicly with an experienced teammate$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague owns a task you delegated and asks you to approve a small decision within the agreed limits. The colleague owns a major outcome involving senior stakeholders and material risks but keeps returning routine choices to you. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Remind them that the decision is within their authority and let them decide$qb$, $qb$Confirm that they can decide and ask them to keep you informed$qb$, $qb$Approve it this time so the work keeps moving$qb$, $qb$Take back decisions that could affect the outcome$qb$, $qb$Require approval for all future decisions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team is busy with several useful activities, but one has little connection to the current goal. Individually valuable requests from powerful stakeholders are collectively displacing the few strategic outcomes already agreed. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Reduce or stop the low-value activity and redirect effort to the goal$qb$, $qb$Review whether the activity still has a useful purpose$qb$, $qb$Keep it because the team has already invested time$qb$, $qb$Continue until the next planning cycle$qb$, $qb$Add more people so all activities can continue$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another team will be affected by a change you are making tomorrow. The stakeholder's changes partly reflect genuine market movement but are also creating avoidable churn across multiple teams. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Tell them before the change, explain the effect, and check what they need$qb$, $qb$Send them a short advance notice about the change$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager and assume the message will reach them$qb$, $qb$Inform them after the change is complete$qb$, $qb$Wait until they notice the effect$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A junior teammate gives the right answer but relied on a guess. You need the person to handle unfamiliar high-impact decisions without creating dependence on your approval. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to work through the reasoning so they can repeat the judgement reliably$qb$, $qb$Ask what information they would check next time$qb$, $qb$Praise the correct answer and move on$qb$, $qb$Tell them guessing is unacceptable$qb$, $qb$Make future decisions for them$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A delegated task is on track, but the colleague chooses a different sequence from yours. The colleague owns a major outcome involving senior stakeholders and material risks but keeps returning routine choices to you. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Focus on the agreed result and intervene only if the different sequence creates a real risk$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the sequence and continue if it meets the requirements$qb$, $qb$Tell them to follow your sequence because you know the task$qb$, $qb$Review every step before they proceed$qb$, $qb$Take the task back$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team is offered an extra activity that sounds useful but does not support the current priority. Individually valuable requests from powerful stakeholders are collectively displacing the few strategic outcomes already agreed. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Compare its value with the priority and decline or defer it if it distracts from the goal$qb$, $qb$Ask whether it can be scheduled after priority work$qb$, $qb$Accept it because useful opportunities should not be missed$qb$, $qb$Add it without reducing any existing work$qb$, $qb$Do it first because it is new$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You learn that a change may affect a colleague's deadline, but the exact impact is not yet known. The stakeholder's changes partly reflect genuine market movement but also create avoidable churn across several teams. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Give an early heads-up, explain what is known, and promise a specific follow-up$qb$, $qb$Tell them there may be an impact and when you expect clarity$qb$, $qb$Wait until the impact is fully confirmed$qb$, $qb$Tell only your manager$qb$, $qb$Say nothing unless the deadline actually moves$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new teammate solves a problem slowly but correctly. You need the person to handle unfamiliar high-impact decisions without creating dependence on your approval. What is the best coaching step?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain their method and help them find one safe way to make it faster$qb$, $qb$Review the steps with them and identify one improvement$qb$, $qb$Tell them the expected time and ask them to speed up$qb$, $qb$Show them your method and require them to copy it$qb$, $qb$Take over similar work$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a task and the colleague identifies a small risk you had not considered. The colleague owns a major outcome involving senior stakeholders and material risks but keeps returning routine choices to you. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the risk, adjust the boundaries if needed, and let them continue ownership$qb$, $qb$Thank them for raising it and agree how to manage it$qb$, $qb$Take the task back because the risk was unexpected$qb$, $qb$Tell them to follow the original plan anyway$qb$, $qb$Ignore the risk because it is small$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new request is useful but would delay work tied directly to the team's main goal. Individually valuable requests from powerful stakeholders are collectively displacing the few strategic outcomes already agreed. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Protect the goal-critical work and negotiate timing for the new request$qb$, $qb$Explain the conflict and offer a later delivery for the new request$qb$, $qb$Accept both and ask the team to work faster$qb$, $qb$Do the new request first because someone asked for it$qb$, $qb$Drop the goal-critical work without discussion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague expects a result today, but you discover a dependency may delay it. The stakeholder's changes partly reflect genuine market movement but also create avoidable churn across several teams. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Tell them early, explain the dependency, and agree what update or alternative they need$qb$, $qb$Warn them that the result may be late and give the next update time$qb$, $qb$Wait until the delay is certain$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager but not the colleague$qb$, $qb$Say nothing until the original deadline passes$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A junior teammate completes a task correctly only after asking for help at every step. You need the person to handle unfamiliar high-impact decisions without creating dependence on your approval. What is the best coaching response?$qb$, $qb$Review the task with them, identify where confidence is low, and let them handle the next similar task with agreed check-ins$qb$, $qb$Ask them to try the next task first and come to you at defined points$qb$, $qb$Continue answering every question as it comes$qb$, $qb$Give the task to someone more independent$qb$, $qb$Tell them to stop asking questions$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague you delegated to makes a reasonable decision within the agreed limits but chooses differently from you. The colleague owns a major outcome involving senior stakeholders and material risks but keeps returning routine choices to you. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Support the decision if it meets the outcome and limits, and discuss the reasoning afterward if useful$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the choice and confirm it still meets the goal$qb$, $qb$Reverse the decision because you would have chosen differently$qb$, $qb$Require approval for similar decisions next time$qb$, $qb$Take back the task$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your team has capacity for only one of two improvements. One directly supports the current goal; the other is easier to deliver. Individually valuable requests from powerful stakeholders are collectively displacing the few strategic outcomes already agreed. Which should you choose?$qb$, $qb$The improvement that contributes more to the current goal$qb$, $qb$The goal-linked improvement after confirming its expected impact$qb$, $qb$The easier improvement because it can show a quick win$qb$, $qb$Split effort between both$qb$, $qb$Choose the one most people prefer$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are changing a process that will make another person's weekly report arrive later. The stakeholder's changes partly reflect genuine market movement but also create avoidable churn across several teams. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Discuss the impact before the change and agree how their reporting need will be met$qb$, $qb$Tell them the new timing and ask whether it creates a problem$qb$, $qb$Make the change and send them an update afterward$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager and let them handle the communication$qb$, $qb$Make the change because your process is more efficient$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A junior colleague fixes an error after you point it out but does not understand why it happened. The goal is to build independent judgement for unfamiliar, high-impact decisions. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Help them identify the cause and the check that would prevent the same error next time$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain what they will do differently next time$qb$, $qb$Tell them to be more careful$qb$, $qb$Check all their future work yourself$qb$, $qb$Move the task to someone else$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks permission for a routine choice that is clearly within the authority you gave them. The delegated outcome involves senior stakeholders and material risks, but routine choices should not keep returning to you. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm that they can make the choice and reinforce the agreed decision limits$qb$, $qb$Remind them of their authority and ask them to proceed$qb$, $qb$Approve the choice this time$qb$, $qb$Take over the decision because they asked$qb$, $qb$Require approval for all future routine choices$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A proposed task would improve a minor internal process but delay an important customer commitment. Individually valuable requests from powerful stakeholders are collectively displacing agreed strategic outcomes. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Protect the customer commitment and schedule the internal improvement when capacity allows$qb$, $qb$Explain the trade-off and defer the internal task$qb$, $qb$Try to do both at the same time$qb$, $qb$Do the internal improvement because it is quick$qb$, $qb$Delay the customer work without discussion$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A person asks for more detail in every update, but you are unsure what they actually use. The changes partly reflect genuine market movement but also create avoidable churn across several teams. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask what decisions they make from the update and tailor the detail to that need$qb$, $qb$Ask which sections they want expanded$qb$, $qb$Add more detail to every update$qb$, $qb$Send the full working file instead$qb$, $qb$Tell them the current update is sufficient$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A capable employee repeatedly solves problems themselves instead of developing their team. Results remain good, but team dependency is increasing. What coaching response is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Use specific examples to show the dependency risk, explore why they step in, and agree measurable behaviors that shift ownership to the team.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to delegate more and review progress at the next performance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Reduce their workload so they have more time to coach others.$qb$, $qb$Promote a stronger team member who can absorb more responsibility.$qb$, $qb$Accept the style because current results are strong.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a high-stakes workstream to a strong manager. Which setup best balances empowerment and control?$qb$, $qb$Agree outcomes, decision boundaries, escalation triggers and review points, then let the manager choose the operating approach.$qb$, $qb$Give the manager full authority and step in only if results deteriorate.$qb$, $qb$Specify the key tasks and require weekly approval before major actions.$qb$, $qb$Keep the important stakeholder decisions with yourself while delegating execution.$qb$, $qb$Ask for daily updates so you remain closely informed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A division has five strategic priorities, but investment and leadership attention are spread almost evenly across twelve initiatives. What is the best corrective action?$qb$, $qb$Translate the priorities into explicit resource choices, stop or defer misaligned work, and make the consequences visible to sponsors.$qb$, $qb$Remind leaders that the five priorities should receive more attention.$qb$, $qb$Rank all twelve initiatives and continue them in priority order.$qb$, $qb$Ask each function to identify which initiatives it considers strategic.$qb$, $qb$Increase the strategic-priority list so more active initiatives are formally included.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A powerful stakeholder supports your initiative publicly but privately blocks resources because another project benefits their function more. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Surface the conflicting incentives directly, clarify enterprise consequences, and negotiate an explicit resource decision with the true trade-off visible.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the stakeholder's behavior to their manager with evidence.$qb$, $qb$Reduce your resource request so the stakeholder can support both projects.$qb$, $qb$Continue seeking informal support from other leaders until enough resources appear.$qb$, $qb$Avoid confronting the conflict because the stakeholder's public support is still useful.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A respected executive solves difficult problems personally and delivers results, but successors are not developing. How should you coach them?$qb$, $qb$Show how personal problem-solving constrains bench strength, agree outcomes they must enable through others, and review evidence of capability growth over time.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to spend more time mentoring high-potential employees.$qb$, $qb$Reduce their operational workload for development conversations.$qb$, $qb$Nominate their best people for external leadership programs.$qb$, $qb$Leave the approach unchanged because results are strong.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are accountable for a major program but have appointed an experienced program director. Which decision should remain primarily with you?$qb$, $qb$Setting outcome boundaries and risk appetite while giving the director authority over delivery choices within them.$qb$, $qb$Approving every material supplier and staffing decision.$qb$, $qb$Running weekly workstream reviews for direct visibility.$qb$, $qb$Choosing detailed milestone sequence and dependencies.$qb$, $qb$Resolving routine disagreements between workstream leads.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A profitable legacy business funds current earnings while a new model is strategically necessary but initially dilutive. What is the strongest alignment approach?$qb$, $qb$Define the transition thesis, specify how the legacy business funds and enables the future model, and use distinct measures and milestones for both horizons.$qb$, $qb$Set one common growth target so both are judged consistently.$qb$, $qb$Prioritize the new model immediately because strategy outweighs earnings.$qb$, $qb$Protect the legacy business until the new model proves profitability.$qb$, $qb$Let each business pursue its own objectives and reconcile at year-end.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A regulator wants rapid remediation, customers want uninterrupted service, and investors focus on margin. Your solution cannot maximize all three. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Map obligations, interests and non-negotiables; develop transparent trade-offs; recommend an option; and engage each stakeholder around the decision.$qb$, $qb$Prioritize the regulator because legal authority outweighs other interests.$qb$, $qb$Choose the lowest-cost compliant solution and explain service impact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the board to decide so dissatisfaction sits at the highest level.$qb$, $qb$Communicate different versions of the plan to emphasize what each group values.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A vice president consistently gives solutions whenever team members bring problems, creating speed but little independent judgment. What coaching focus is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Help the leader shift from solving to framing, questioning and setting decision boundaries, then review whether the team increasingly decides well without escalation.$qb$, $qb$Introduce a sensible safeguard but leave the underlying incentives or decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Run a small corrective action focused mainly on the most visible symptom.$qb$, $qb$Ask individual managers to compensate for the problem through extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Treat the issue as unavoidable and make no material change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a major operating redesign to a trusted executive. Midway through, several peers lobby you to change details directly. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Route substantive challenges through the delegated leader and agreed governance, intervening only when boundaries, risk appetite or enterprise outcomes require it.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate issue with a reasonable local action, but leave the broader assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather some additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask the affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or externally forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A move upmarket is generating strong revenue but pulling capacity away from the company's most defensible core segment. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Revisit strategic logic and resource boundaries, evaluate whether the growth strengthens or erodes the intended advantage, and reallocate based on enterprise value.$qb$, $qb$Take a practical partial step and escalate the remaining uncertainty to a higher authority.$qb$, $qb$Apply the existing policy more carefully, even though it does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects the most visible short-term target.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the conflicting evidence and continue with the established position.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A community coalition opposes a new facility despite regulatory approval, while local government strongly supports the jobs it creates. What is the strongest approach?$qb$, $qb$Understand distinct interests and credible impacts, engage transparently, explore mitigations, and keep legal approval separate from social acceptance.$qb$, $qb$Act on the strongest available signal while keeping a later review point.$qb$, $qb$Seek broader consensus before making a limited decision.$qb$, $qb$Use a familiar precedent even though important conditions differ.$qb$, $qb$Choose the easiest path to implement regardless of longer-term consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior leader's team performs well only when that leader is personally involved in major decisions. What should you coach first?$qb$, $qb$Help the leader define decision principles and boundaries, transfer real authority deliberately, and review decision quality without their intervention.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegated a sensitive restructuring, but employees bypass the appointed executive and appeal directly to you. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Reinforce the executive's authority, route issues through agreed channels, and personally handle only matters outside the delegation's boundaries.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A move upmarket grows revenue but increasingly depends on discounts that weaken the premium position the board approved. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Reconcile growth with strategic position by examining customer economics, discount drivers and resource choices, then reset boundaries and measures.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A partner, regulator, employee group and investor each want different outcomes from a planned business-model change. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Map obligations, interests, influence and likely reactions, distinguish negotiable from non-negotiable issues, and sequence engagement around actual trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A highly capable executive's successors keep leaving because they receive responsibility but little real authority. What should you coach?$qb$, $qb$Help the executive identify where they reclaim decisions, redesign authority boundaries and consequences, and measure whether successors can actually lead outcomes independently.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a major client relationship to a successor, but the client continues calling you for every important decision. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Reinforce the successor's authority with the client, remain available within explicit escalation boundaries, and avoid solving issues that properly belong to the new owner.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy promises both premium differentiation and aggressive market-share growth, but operating choices increasingly force a trade-off. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Make the conflict explicit, clarify which customers and economics the strategy prioritizes, and reset measures and resource choices so the organization is not pursuing incompatible aims.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A proposed plant closure satisfies financial targets but would severely affect a community that is also a major customer base. What is the strongest stakeholder approach?$qb$, $qb$Integrate financial, workforce, customer, community and reputational consequences into options, engage affected groups appropriately, and make trade-offs transparent to decision owners.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A leader you coach has strong successors on paper, but they are never allowed to represent the function at high-stakes forums. What should you address?$qb$, $qb$Create deliberate exposure with real authority and appropriate support, and examine what risk or identity concern keeps the leader from letting successors operate visibly.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$At first, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$In this case, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a sensitive regulatory remediation to an experienced executive. A regulator then asks to deal only with you. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Respect the regulator's legitimate access while keeping the executive visibly accountable for the work, clarifying which issues require your direct involvement and which remain delegated.$qb$, $qb$At first, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$In this case, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy depends on serving fewer, higher-value customers, but sales incentives still reward account volume. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Align incentives, capacity and customer-selection rules with the strategic choice so teams are not rewarded for behavior that undermines the intended model.$qb$, $qb$In this case, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$At first, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A large restructuring affects employees, suppliers, local government and customers differently across regions. What is the strongest engagement design?$qb$, $qb$Segment stakeholders by obligations, impact and influence, coordinate core facts and principles, and tailor timing and dialogue without making inconsistent commitments.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$At first, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$In this case, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A respected executive develops talented people, but they routinely move to other functions because there are too few senior roles locally. How should you coach them?$qb$, $qb$Treat enterprise talent flow as a leadership success, build successors continuously, and align recognition so exporting talent is not penalized.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though it does not fully fit the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a major supplier recovery to a vice president, but another executive asks you to approve a side agreement directly. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Route it through the vice president and agreed governance, intervening directly only if it crosses the authority or risk boundaries you retained.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral decision that reduces visible tension while leaving the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your strategy emphasizes recurring revenue, but a large one-time deal would consume capacity needed for subscription customers and help this year's target. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate the deal against the strategic economic model and opportunity cost, and make an explicit exception decision rather than letting annual targets override strategy.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer the less visible consequences until results can be measured.$qb$, $qb$Use a partial pilot without specifying the evidence or threshold that would determine the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operational team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects this year's reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A major automation program improves competitiveness but eliminates roles in communities where your company is a major employer. What is the strongest approach?$qb$, $qb$Integrate workforce, community, customer and economic impacts into transition options, engage affected groups early enough to shape mitigations, and make trade-offs explicit.$qb$, $qb$Take a sensible immediate action on the visible issue and schedule the broader structural question for the next governance review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the resulting consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current course until the risk becomes certain enough to force action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior leader says they empower their team but routinely reverse decisions when outcomes differ from what they would have chosen. What should you coach?$qb$, $qb$Clarify which outcomes versus methods matter, establish decision boundaries and tolerance for acceptable variation, and stop reclaiming authority merely because choices differ.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, although it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior ruling before fully examining the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if risk or cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a high-profile client negotiation, but the client asks you privately for an assurance that contradicts your negotiator's position. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Avoid negotiating around your delegate, understand the concern, and route any change through the agreed negotiation authority and escalation boundaries.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek more stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy prioritizes profitable growth, but leaders interpret this as refusing investments that dilute margin temporarily. What should leadership clarify?$qb$, $qb$Define the time horizon, economic logic and acceptable investment profile behind profitable growth so short-term margin does not become a substitute for strategy.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot but leave the success threshold and next decision undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A new facility offers strong economics but requires relocating a community and creates significant environmental concerns beyond legal minimums. What is the strongest approach?$qb$, $qb$Integrate social, environmental, legal and economic consequences into alternatives, engage affected stakeholders meaningfully, and make residual trade-offs explicit to decision owners.$qb$, $qb$Take a useful immediate step on the visible issue, but defer the wider structural question to the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until the risk becomes unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior executive delegates decisions but frequently signals afterward that they would have chosen differently, causing employees to seek informal pre-approval. What should you coach?$qb$, $qb$Help the executive distinguish unacceptable outcomes from acceptable variation, stop second-guessing sound delegated choices, and reinforce authority through consistent behavior.$qb$, $qb$Apply the nearest existing precedent with an added safeguard, although it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the quickest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a sensitive partner negotiation, but your board chair asks you privately for concessions the negotiator has rejected. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the chair's concern and governance authority, then route any change through the agreed negotiation mandate rather than undermining the delegated negotiator informally.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy calls for simplification, but teams interpret this as cutting features, reducing staff or standardizing processes indiscriminately. What should leadership clarify?$qb$, $qb$Define the customer and economic outcomes simplification is meant to create, the choices it implies, and boundaries that prevent the slogan from substituting for strategic judgment.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A planned facility meets legal requirements but local communities raise credible concerns about cumulative water use that permits do not fully address. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Assess the broader impact and alternatives, engage affected stakeholders and experts, incorporate mitigations into the decision, and make residual trade-offs explicit.$qb$, $qb$Take a sensible immediate action on the visible issue and defer the broader structural question to the next governance review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until the risk becomes unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A leader delegates budget authority but routinely questions every expenditure afterward, causing managers to seek pre-clearance. What should you coach?$qb$, $qb$Set clear spending boundaries and outcomes, review patterns rather than second-guessing reasonable individual choices, and reinforce managers who exercise sound delegated judgment.$qb$, $qb$Apply the nearest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the quickest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a major transformation to an executive, but a board member begins sending detailed instructions directly to the project team. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Respect the board member's oversight concerns while restoring the agreed governance channel so direction flows through accountable leadership rather than bypassing delegated authority.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy says the company will be 'customer-led,' and functions interpret this as accepting almost every customer request. What should leadership clarify?$qb$, $qb$Define which customers and needs the strategy prioritizes, how value and economics guide choices, and where saying no protects the intended proposition.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A proposed mine expansion is economically attractive and legally permitted but could materially affect water availability for nearby communities. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Assess cumulative impacts and alternatives, engage affected communities and experts before irreversible commitment, and make residual economic and social trade-offs explicit.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue and defer the broader structural question to the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until the risk becomes unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R10-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A leader delegates hiring decisions but routinely rejects candidates chosen by managers for subjective 'fit' reasons. What should you coach?$qb$, $qb$Clarify role-relevant selection criteria and retained veto boundaries, require evidence for intervention, and let managers exercise authority when choices meet agreed standards.$qb$, $qb$Apply the nearest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the quickest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R10-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a major integration to an executive, but the CEO asks you informally to change a workstream leader without involving them. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify the CEO's concern and authority, then address any change through the accountable integration leader and governance rather than undermining delegated ownership.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R10-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy promises 'innovation leadership,' and teams respond by launching many experiments with little connection to customer or economic priorities. What should leadership clarify?$qb$, $qb$Define the strategic problems, domains and value outcomes innovation should address, along with portfolio criteria for funding, learning and stopping work.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R10-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A profitable project meets all legal requirements but would substantially increase traffic and pollution in a vulnerable neighborhood. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Assess broader impacts and alternatives, engage affected stakeholders meaningfully, incorporate mitigations, and make residual trade-offs visible to accountable decision-makers.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue and defer the broader structural question to the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until the risk becomes unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R01-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Several executives you developed are ready for larger roles, but moving them would temporarily weaken your own leadership team. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Optimize for enterprise leadership capacity, release talent with planned succession and transition support, and treat your ability to regenerate strong leaders as part of your own performance.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with additional safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R01-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You delegate a major strategic initiative to an executive who chooses a path different from what you would have chosen, but it remains within agreed boundaries. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Support the delegated choice, monitor agreed outcomes and risks, and intervene only if evidence crosses the boundaries rather than substituting your preference for their authority.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R01-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your stated strategy contains three priorities that cannot all be fully funded after a major economic shock. What should the executive team do?$qb$, $qb$Revisit the strategic choices explicitly, rank what must be protected, stop or defer lower-value commitments, and align resources and measures to the revised priorities.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave the success criteria and subsequent decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask the relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R01-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A decision to exit a country would protect shareholder value but materially affect employees, suppliers and communities that relied on long-term company commitments. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Integrate fiduciary, legal and stakeholder consequences into exit options, engage affected groups responsibly, mitigate avoidable harm and make unavoidable trade-offs explicit.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer the broader structural implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make the need for change undeniable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R02-030$qb$, $qb$A5-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have built an exceptional leadership team, but the board wants two members to move into other businesses immediately. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Support enterprise deployment, manage succession and transition deliberately, and judge your own leadership by the system's ability to regenerate talent rather than preserve a strong local team.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R02-031$qb$, $qb$A5-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A delegated executive makes a high-stakes decision within agreed authority that produces a poor outcome despite sound process. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Stand behind the legitimate delegation, review assumptions and learning without retrospectively punishing sound judgment, and adjust boundaries only if evidence shows they were wrong.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R02-032$qb$, $qb$A5-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A major shock invalidates the economics behind one of your three declared strategic pillars. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Reopen the strategy explicitly, decide what to stop or redefine, reallocate capital and measures, and explain why disciplined adaptation strengthens rather than weakens strategic credibility.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R02-033$qb$, $qb$A5-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your company can exit an unprofitable region quickly, but doing so would abandon long-standing supplier and workforce commitments. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate legal, economic and relational obligations, design a responsible transition that mitigates avoidable harm, and make residual costs part of the exit decision rather than externalizing them.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
